It is considered bad practice because when an exception occurs the object is destroyed and to prevent this we can write the following code:

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() {
        throw std::runtime_error("Exception");
    }
};
```

In the above code, the object will be destroyed when an exception is thrown. To avoid this we can write the following code:

```cpp
class MyClass {
private:
    std::exception exception;
public:
    MyClass() {
        throw std::runtime_error(&exception);
    }

    ~MyClass() {
        if (exception) {
            // If the exception is not handled then do nothing
            // You can throw the exception as well
        }
    }
};
```

